// Bans `instanceof` against this repository's error classes. Each SSR / RSC
// module graph holds its own copy of the constructor, so `instanceof` silently
// evaluates to false across that split. Use the `Symbol.for()` brand +
// `serializedKind` guard functions (`isConflictError`, `isAppServerError`, …).
//
// MAINTENANCE — this matches the identifier as written, so a new error class is
// NOT covered until its name is added below. Nobody has to remember that:
// `lint/banList.test.ts` scans `apps/`, `packages/`, `infra/` and `lint/` for
// class declarations named `*Error` or visibly extending one, and fails naming
// any this list is missing (and any entry here whose class is gone), so the
// omission is loud rather than silent. That makes this list the roster of every
// error class that scan can see — which is not quite every error class; the
// third limit below says which ones it misses, and `banList.test.ts` pins both
// its reach and that gap with direct cases.
//
// WIRING — `lint/pluginWiring.test.ts` lints a throwaway fixture and asserts a
// `category: "plugin"` diagnostic carrying this file's message comes back, so
// deleting `biome.json`'s `plugins` line, dropping the `register_diagnostic(...)`
// call below, or narrowing `linter.includes` off the fixture's root turns red
// instead of quietly disarming everything above. The fixture lives under
// `packages/core/src/` for that last one — see Placement below.
//
// SUPPRESSION — `// biome-ignore lint/plugin: <reason>` on the preceding line
// (the intended use is a negative control asserting that `instanceof` really
// does return false across a duplicated module graph). `// biome-ignore plugin:`
// does not work, and neither does `overrides[].plugins: []` — `overrides` only
// adds plugins, so an empty array is a no-op.
//
// KNOWN LIMITS, accepted:
// - Evasion — a rebinding of any kind (an aliased import —
//   `import { ConflictError as CE }` → `e instanceof CE` — or a local one,
//   `const CE = ConflictError`), a namespace access (`import * as errs` →
//   `e instanceof errs.ConflictError`) and any syntax that wraps the
//   right-hand side in another node — parentheses (`e instanceof
//   (ConflictError)`), a non-null assertion (`e instanceof ConflictError!`) or
//   a type assertion (`e instanceof <new () => Error>ConflictError`; the
//   snippet match does not see through the wrapper node) — all slip through.
//   The rebindings are conspicuous in review; the wrappers are not, so
//   `pluginWiring.test.ts` pins the parenthesized and non-null forms as
//   negative cases rather than doubling every `or` entry with wrapped twins.
// - Placement — the plugin only runs over `biome.json`'s `linter.includes`.
//   Measured: the same `e instanceof ConflictError` yields a diagnostic under
//   `packages/core/src/` but none in a repo-root `*.ts` or under
//   `**/components/ui/**` (nor `**/styles/**` / `**/templates/**`, all
//   subtracted there). Those two blind spots are not the same size: the
//   subtracted paths sit inside roots `banList.test.ts` walks, so a class
//   declared in one still lands on this list and only its call sites go
//   unchecked — a repo-root `*.ts` sits outside those roots too, so a class
//   declared there reaches neither the plugin nor this list. Today the root
//   holds config files only, and `banList.test.ts` asserts that
//   `linter.includes` names no directory root its scan skips, so the gap
//   cannot widen silently. Dropping a root from `linter.includes` disarms the plugin
//   there silently, which is why `pluginWiring.test.ts` lints a fixture under
//   each of `packages/`, `apps/` and `infra/` rather than beside itself; that
//   pins the three main roots one at a time, so any single root falling out of
//   `linter.includes` turns it red.
// - Roster — the scan reads class declarations and named class expressions
//   (`const X = class …`) textually, so a class reaches this list only if the
//   identifier a call site would hold ends in `Error` or the base it names does.
//   The type-parameter list is skipped with a depth counter before the header
//   is read, so a `{` inside a constraint (`class X<T extends { a: string }>`)
//   does not hide the heritage clause and a `T extends Error` constraint does
//   not qualify a non-error class — both pinned in `banList.test.ts`. Three
//   forms therefore never arrive on their own:
//   `export default class extends Error {}` (anonymous), a non-`*Error` name
//   over an aliased base (`import { CodedError as Base }` →
//   `class Weird extends Base`), and a class expression wrapped in a call
//   (`export const X = mixin(class extends CodedError {})` — the scan requires
//   `class` directly after the `=`, so the wrapper hides it). The first is
//   beyond this plugin either way — an anonymous default export gives its call
//   sites no stable identifier to match. The second is a real hole, of the same
//   shape as the alias evasion above and equally conspicuous in review. The
//   third is a hole of a different kind: call sites do hold the stable binding
//   `X`, so the plugin can ban it — but only if the name is added to the list
//   by hand, since the scan never surfaces it. All three are pinned as negative
//   cases in `banList.test.ts`. Every other form is covered: `export class`, `export abstract class`,
//   `export default class Named`, a bare `class X {}` re-exported by a later
//   `export { X }`, and a class expression bound to a `const` / `let` / `var`
//   (`export const X = class extends CodedError {}`).
//
// Why not widen to a regex such as `r"^[A-Za-z0-9_.]*Error$"` plus an allow-list
// (measured to work in Biome's GritQL, and it does close the namespace evasion):
// the allow-list is only `Error` today, but it grows with every builtin or
// third-party `*Error` the repo starts using, and the alias evasion survives the
// switch either way. The one real advantage — that a regex needs no maintenance
// when a class is added — is what `banList.test.ts` already buys.
`$value instanceof $type` where {
  $type <: or {
    // packages/core/src/lib/error.ts
    `CodedError`,
    // packages/core/src/domain/error.ts
    `BusinessRuleError`,
    `RehydrationError`,
    // packages/core/src/application/errors.ts
    `ApplicationError`,
    `NotFoundError`,
    `ConflictError`,
    `ValidationError`,
    `UnauthorizedError`,
    `ForbiddenError`,
    `SystemError`,
    // apps/web/app/presentation/
    `AppServerError`,
    `InputValidationError`
  },
  register_diagnostic(span=$type, message="Use the guard function (isXxxError) instead of instanceof for error type checks.")
}
